 "kind": "inhibitory knot (k=7)",
 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d031: inhibitory knot (k=7)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.1 and radius 1.64 with 7 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.33) and a 4-dim charge; 6 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 24 couplings (67% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 0.84 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.84 bonds per particle, largest molecule 5.0 particles, 13.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.103 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.103  random bonds 0.108\n  xor      0.500 vs ESN 0.514\u00b10.003  [below, at chance]  no bonds 0.471  random bonds 0.562\n  narma10  0.307 vs ESN 0.844\u00b10.001  [below]  no bonds 0.290  random bonds 0.318\n  latch    0.882 vs ESN 0.678\u00b10.003  [beat]  no bonds 0.871  random bonds 0.883\n  mnist14  0.549 vs ESN 0.830\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.516  random bonds 0.557\n\nGood at: latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
